{"maxId":27,"entries":{"chapter-23-study-substance-use-and-disorders|Chapter 23 Study: Substance Use and Disorders":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Unhealthy use includes risk of harm or established health/social consequences; addiction is a health condition, not a moral failure (p317, p321).":["u3"],"copy|Aging, multimorbidity and interacting medicines increase harm, sometimes at lower exposure than in younger adults (p320–321).":["u4"],"copy|A U.S. standard drink contains 14 g ethanol. The chapter cites daily limits of two drinks for men and one for women on drinking days; contraindicating conditions/medications may require abstinence (p317).":["u5"],"copy|Reduced tolerance and changed social roles can obscure substance-use diagnostic criteria in older adults (p322).":["u6"],"copy|Ask nonjudgmentally about alcohol, prescriptions, nonprescription drugs, cannabis and other substances; recommended screening includes older adults (p321–322).":["u7"],"copy|Use AUDIT/AUDIT-C or geriatric tools such as MAST-G and CARET; consider comorbidity and drug interactions, not consumption alone (p322–323).":["u8"],"copy|Positive screens require assessment of use pattern, consequences, severity, supports and treatment needs (p323).":["u9"],"copy|Brief interventions, motivational approaches and psychosocial treatment can help; tailor goals and supports to the person (p323–324).":["u10"],"copy|Alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal may need closely monitored specialty care (p324).":["u11"],"copy|Naltrexone: oral daily or monthly injection; contraindicated with prescribed opioids, acute hepatitis or liver failure (p324).":["u12"],"copy|Acamprosate: oral three times daily; reduce for renal insufficiency. Evidence is strongest after initial abstinence in younger-adult trials (p324).":["u13"],"copy|Disulfiram: generally not recommended in older adults because the alcohol reaction can be harmful; if used, great caution is required (p324).":["u14"],"copy|Opioid-use disorder has medication options including methadone, buprenorphine and naltrexone; combine medication access with supportive care (p324–326).":["u15"],"copy|The chapter has limited older-adult trial evidence for many medications; use its source dose table and individual precautions (p324–325).":["u16"],"copy|Continue follow-up and reassessment; recurrence can occur in the course of chronic substance-use disorders (p321).":["u17"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u18"],"copy|CHAPTER 23s / HAZZARD":["u19"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 23; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u20"],"copy|Identify unhealthy use":["u21"],"copy|Screen & assess":["u22"],"copy|Treatment":["u23"],"copy|No past-exam questions are currently linked to this chapter.":["u24"],"copy|CHAPTER 23 / HAZZARD":["u19"]},"sequenceOffsets":[[28,-3],[31,0]]}
